# Frontend Vue Standards

For color, spacing, typography, elevation, and motion, follow
`frontend-design-system.mdc`.

## SFC block order

- Always keep Vue SFC blocks in this exact order:
  1) `<script setup lang="ts">` (or `<script>`)
  2) `<template>`
  3) `<style scoped lang="scss">` (or `<style>`)

## CSS naming (BEM)

- Use BEM for all classes in Vue styles:
  - Block: `.chat-card`
  - Element: `.chat-card__title`
  - Modifier: `.chat-card--active`
- Avoid generic class names like `.wrapper`, `.item`, `.left`.

## Atomic design component layering

- Follow Atomic Design when adding or refactoring UI:
  - Atoms: primitive UI (`Button`, `Icon`, `Input`)
  - Molecules: simple combinations (`SearchField`, `UserBadge`)
  - Organisms: section-level composites (`ChatSidebar`, `MessageList`)
  - Views/pages compose organisms and data flow.
- Prefer placing reusable UI in atomic component folders before adding logic to views.

## File length limit

- Keep `.vue` files under 300 lines.
- If a file grows beyond 300 lines, split by responsibility:
  - Extract child presentational components
  - Extract composables for reusable state/logic
  - Extract constants/helpers to separate modules

## Prop formatting and spacing

- Break long tag attributes into one-per-line when there are 3+ props or lines become hard to scan.
- Keep readable indentation and consistent padding/spacing in template and styles.

```vue
<!-- Preferred multiline prop formatting -->
<ChatMessage
  :author="message.author"
  :timestamp="message.createdAt"
  :is-own="message.authorId === currentUserId"
  :text="message.body"
/>
```

## Additional standards to enforce

- Prefer composables for shared logic instead of mixins.
- Use scoped styles by default; only use global styles intentionally.
- Define explicit `emits` and typed props.
- Keep side effects in lifecycle/composables, not inline in template expressions.
